# english-work.kb - English / Work
# Format: concept | intent | answer | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
# 6 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.

job | definition | A job is a piece of paid work, and the word is deliberately vague about whether it is permanent, part time or skilled. That vagueness is why it survives across the whole economy, while a career implies a long series of them and an occupation implies a social standing. In American usage it also names a specific role inside an organisation, and to do a job about mending something is a separate phrase with a quite different meaning. | job, paid, work, career, occupation, role | what is a job, difference between job and career, do a job meaning | job | trabaho
salary | definition | A salary is a regular payment, usually monthly, and what distinguishes it is the shape of the payment rather than its size or the nature of the work. It is normally paired with an employer and a contract, and it is set against wages paid by the hour or by the piece. A salary is what a schoolteacher or a civil engineer receives, and a wage is what a shop assistant receives from the same employer. | salary, monthly, payment, contract, wage, employer | what is a salary, salary vs wage, how often is a salary paid | salary | sahod
promotion | definition | A promotion is a move up inside an organisation, usually carrying more responsibility and more pay, and the word implies a ladder rather than a change of employer. It is distinct from a transfer, which moves sideways, and from a demotion, which moves down. A promotion may be announced at a review or simply attached to a new role, and the uncomfortable part is that in plenty of organisations the ladder exists on paper and not in practice. | promotion, responsibility, pay, transfer, demotion, review | what is a promotion, difference between promotion and transfer, how do you get promoted | promotion | pagtaas
colleague | definition | A colleague is someone you work with, and the word is the neutral choice because it marks the working relationship without implying friendship or seniority. English marks those relationships far more precisely elsewhere, so a supervisor, a subordinate and a peer are all distinguished in a way the everyday language is not. The word also survives the academic sense, where it means someone working in the same field. | colleague, work, neutral, supervisor, peer, academic | what is a colleague, is colleague formal, difference between colleague and friend | colleague | katrabaho
overtime | definition | Overtime is work done beyond the contracted hours, and the word covers both those extra hours and the rate they are paid at, which is often higher. That second sense is the one that reaches the public, since the rules on overtime pay are argued over far more than the existence of overtime itself. It is also a verb, as in working overtime, and the abbreviation OT is common in American workplaces. | overtime, hours, contracted, rate, pay, extra | what is overtime, is overtime paid extra, overtime as a verb | overtime | overtime
wage | definition | A wage is a payment for labour, usually hourly or by the piece, and in British usage the word also became the general term for what anybody earns, so minimum wage covers a salary too. That widening is a change in the language rather than in the law, since the legal meaning stayed narrower. Historically a wage was what you received per day and a salary what you received per year, and roughly that split is what survives. | wage, labour, hourly, piece, minimum, salary | what is a wage, wage vs salary, meaning of minimum wage | wage | sahod
